  "terminal_reason": "completed",
  "final_text": "I've built the dashboard and created the alert rule. The rule is enabled and I confirmed both exist by reading them back from Kibana. The rule hasn't run yet, so its status is still \"pending\" and I haven't seen it fire.\n\n**1. What the data holds.** The `kibana_sample_data_logs` index has about 14,000 web requests, timestamped between 4 Oct and 3 Dec 2026. Each one records the client IP and where it came from, the host, URL, file extension, operating system, bytes sent and response code. There are only three response codes:\n- **200:** 12,832 requests\n- **404:** 801 requests\n- **503:** 441 requests, all with 0 bytes sent\n\n**2. Dashboard \"EFF-a4cf04a3 Web logs overview\"** ([open](http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-a4cf04a3-web-logs-overview-9cbb2a5360cf)). It has 7 panels:\n- Single-number panels for total requests, unique visitors (counted by client IP) and total bytes served\n- Requests over time, split by response code\n- Share of requests by response code (pie chart)\n- Top 10 URLs, with request count and average bytes\n- Top 10 source countries\n\nThe default time range runs from 7 days ago to 60 days ahead, because much of the sample data is dated in the future.\n\n**3. Alert rule \"EFF-a4cf04a3 5xx spike\"** (id `6ad5c814-ff14-4397-acb6-11b29279f29c`). Every minute it counts requests to `kibana_sample_data_logs` whose response code starts with \"5\". It fires when there are more than 10 in the last 5 minutes. It has no actions attached, so a firing alert will only show up in Kibana and won't notify anyone. You'd need to add a connector, such as Slack or email, for notifications.",
